{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdsJ5Vx14JENrUk8Hiu7h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PBqvYyL4rEdUifekD71nSd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-creator-0/f185e5e6-15ee-5d4d-b18e-9b212cd19c12/scratchpad/e5/t-031/child-ws/child-result.json","content":"{\n  \"encoder_file\": \"clean_impl.py\",\n  \"undetermined_behaviors\": [\n    \"Entry ordering: format.md never states the order in which entries are emitted. Probe {\\\"b_key\\\":\\\"2\\\",\\\"a_key\\\":\\\"1\\\",\\\"c_key\\\":\\\"3\\\"} is not in sorted key order, so insertion order and sorted order give different output. I chose the dict's own iteration order (insertion order).\",\n    \"Treatment of literal backslashes in a value: format.md only mandates escaping the characters ';' and '=', and is silent about backslash. Probes {\\\"back\\\":\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\"}, {\\\"path\\\":\\\"C:\\\\\\\\tmp\\\\\\\\data\\\",...}, and {\\\"msg\\\":\\\"escape \\\\\\\\; literally\\\",...} contain literal backslashes. A reader could reasonably escape backslashes too (for round-trippability), producing e.g. 'back=\\\\\\\\'; I implemented the literal spec and leave backslashes untouched, so a single backslash is emitted verbatim and 'escape \\\\; literally' becomes 'escape \\\\\\\\; literally' (an ambiguous sequence on any hypothetical decode).\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"format.md defines four rules: entries rendered as key=value, joined by ';', and the value-only escaping of ';' and '=' by a preceding backslash. I implemented exactly these and nothing more. Escaping is applied only to values, not keys (no probe key contains ';' or '='). The two gaps above are the only behaviors probes exercise that the text does not uniquely determine; empty values (probe {\\\"single\\\":\\\"\\\"}) are determined -> 'single='.\"\n}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_toke